  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy conditions will continue across the area \nthrough Monday. \n\nAn upper-low over the Gulf of Maine will continue to track eastward \naway from the region today. Another upper-low centered over eastern \nportions of Ontario today will shift southeastward through Monday, \nwith a sharp trough axis with strong shortwave energy pivoting \nthrough the region by Monday afternoon. At the surface, low pressure \nwill pass north of the area on Monday with a trailing cold front \nmoving through the region. High pressure will begin nudging into the \nregion from the southwest by Monday evening. \n\nA somewhat tight pressure gradient exists over the area this \nafternoon with a surface low to the northeast and a high centered to \nthe southwest. In addition, it is warming into the low 60s across \nthe area, with steep low-level lapse rates in place. Both \nfactors are contributing to breezy west-northwest winds across \nthe area, with gusts generally in the 20-25 mph range. This \nevening, winds will relax as the pressure gradient eases a bit \nand an inversion sets in. Winds overnight should be around 10 \nmph with lows in the upper 40s to mid 40s. \n\nOn Monday, winds aloft will be stronger than they are today, with \nthe pressure gradient remaining fairly tight. Under similar mixing \nconditions, winds Monday will likely be even breezier than they are \ntoday, with gusts up to 30-35 mph possible. No wind headlines should \nbe needed, but the gusty winds will certainly be noticeable. With \nmuch of the region near or past past peak fall foliage, these breezy \nconditions will likely defoliate much of the seasonal vegetation \nacross the area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A coastal storm/nor'easter for our region Thursday \nthrough Friday night for our region.\n\nA vigorous upper-level trough is forecast to evolve across the \neastern U.S. later this week and into the weekend. This would \nsupport a surface low that may initially track near the Ohio Valley, \nbefore a secondary low develops in the vicinity of the northern Mid-\nAtlantic. The model guidance, including ensemble data, continues to \nbe in good agreement that a deep upper-level trough evolves in the \nEast later this week. There are however some differences with the \nhandling of the embedded shortwaves and thus the details of the \nsurface low. The details will be important because they will \ndetermine where the surface low tracks and where the main forcing \nfor ascent becomes established along with some colder air on the \nwest/northwest side of the developing coastal storm/nor'easter.\n\nThe strength of the parent upper-level trough would result in a \ncolder air mass moving in with time, however the initial air mass is \nnot looking all that cold with the lack of a cold antecedent air \nmass. As a result, dynamic cooling would have to come into play. \nThis could allow for the air mass to become sufficiently cold enough \nfor a rain/snow mix or even some snow across our far western to \nnorthwestern zones (elevation dependent). While some guidance is \ncolder and farther south and east (some much farther south and east \nand drier), it does appear at this time that the greatest \nprobabilities for some snow is across the I-80 corridor (Poconos). \nOtherwise, a decent rain event remains possible. The amount of \nprecipitation (rain and/or snow) will depend on the ultimate \ndevelopment and track of the main surface low and the associated \nthermal profiles that evolve across our region. The GEFS and ECMWF \nmembers suggest a spread still with the placement/track of the \nsurface low. The coastal impacts, including the potential for \nstronger winds and also tidal flooding, are less certain because if \nthe surface low tracks right over our area that would lessen the \nresidence time of the (stronger) onshore flow. The uncertainty \nremains with the details for Thursday through Friday night, \ntherefore this time frame will continue to be monitored. It does \nlook to turn much colder though Friday and especially over the \nweekend.",
